WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.865 --> 00:00:05.637
A team lead at a grid operator asks for a better dashboard for the work orders:

2
00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:07.297
a screen full of figures.

3
00:00:08.032 --> 00:00:11.539
Clients rarely bring a problem, but a solution or a symptom.

4
00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:13.799
Separate three layers.

5
00:00:14.528 --> 00:00:16.119
The symptom: what they see.

6
00:00:16.801 --> 00:00:20.714
The problem: the cause underneath, which a project can change.

7
00:00:21.403 --> 00:00:23.795
The goal: what should be measurably different.

8
00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:25.382
What for?

9
00:00:26.076 --> 00:00:27.852
Seeing which jobs get stuck.

10
00:00:28.624 --> 00:00:34.453
140 of 600 orders have waited over 4 weeks, 90 for materials.

11
00:00:35.453 --> 00:00:38.170
The problem: materials aren't linked to the planning.

12
00:00:38.879 --> 00:00:42.432
The goal: no order waits over 4 weeks for materials.

13
00:00:43.137 --> 00:00:47.514
The dashboard becomes a small part: a project on the symptom fixes nothing.

14
00:00:48.821 --> 00:00:52.361
Ask how they notice it, since when, and what has been tried.

15
00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:57.506
Walk through one recent case: it shows the mechanism, not their opinion.

16
00:00:58.508 --> 00:01:01.179
What does it cost per week, or after a year?

17
00:01:01.944 --> 00:01:04.881
That is the cost of inaction: is a project worth it?

18
00:01:05.935 --> 00:01:08.094
Often it is a target on a KPI,

19
00:01:08.269 --> 00:01:13.482
like OEE: the share of planned time a machine makes good parts at full speed.

20
00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:20.187
From 58 to 75 percent is still a wish; 85 is called world class,

21
00:01:20.304 --> 00:01:21.407
but is a convention.

22
00:01:22.126 --> 00:01:27.037
Where do the 42 points go: breakdowns, changeovers, slow running or scrap?

23
00:01:28.343 --> 00:01:31.071
Whoever asks often has their name tied to it.

24
00:01:31.808 --> 00:01:34.223
Don't attack it: a dashboard could well help,

25
00:01:34.397 --> 00:01:36.812
but can I first see where the delays come from?

26
00:01:37.835 --> 00:01:38.973
Analysed already?

27
00:01:39.639 --> 00:01:40.672
Great, show me.

28
00:01:41.372 --> 00:01:43.705
If they can't, you've learned something too.

29
00:01:44.726 --> 00:01:46.189
Experts disagree here.

30
00:01:46.934 --> 00:01:51.938
A fair middle: their solution is a hypothesis you check with data in a week or two.

31
00:01:52.914 --> 00:01:56.652
Ask naive questions, and write their words down literally.

32
00:01:57.932 --> 00:02:02.355
The first conversation: an hour with the production manager of a dairy plant.

33
00:02:03.121 --> 00:02:07.521
Open 5 minutes, explore 35, check 10, close 10.

34
00:02:08.534 --> 00:02:12.690
Say the structure: I'd like to mostly listen and end with a next step.

35
00:02:13.407 --> 00:02:14.974
It puts people at ease.

36
00:02:16.032 --> 00:02:17.367
Explore: their story.

37
00:02:18.066 --> 00:02:20.005
Check: summarise, what's missing?

38
00:02:20.716 --> 00:02:22.411
Close: a step with a date.

39
00:02:23.781 --> 00:02:27.577
Active listening means listening to understand, not to reply.

40
00:02:28.262 --> 00:02:31.583
The checklist MORE PIES comes from crisis negotiation.

41
00:02:32.599 --> 00:02:34.317
M, mood: read the room.

42
00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.203
O: open questions: how does a normal Monday go?

43
00:02:38.939 --> 00:02:41.400
R: reflecting: that sounds frustrating.

44
00:02:42.168 --> 00:02:43.166
E: empathy.

45
00:02:44.189 --> 00:02:47.324
P: paraphrasing, the core in your own words.

46
00:02:48.018 --> 00:02:49.214
I: inquiry.

47
00:02:49.922 --> 00:02:52.894
E: your experience, briefly, for trust.

48
00:02:53.684 --> 00:02:55.762
S: three seconds of silence.

49
00:02:56.743 --> 00:03:01.503
For negotiators, M is minimal encouragers, and S is summarising.

50
00:03:02.855 --> 00:03:06.187
Let the client talk 70 to 80 percent of the time.

51
00:03:06.871 --> 00:03:08.171
Talked for two minutes?

52
00:03:08.884 --> 00:03:09.882
Ask a question.

53
00:03:10.898 --> 00:03:13.534
6 changeovers a day, of 50 minutes.

54
00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:18.535
You paraphrase: so five hours of changeovers block the extra volume?

55
00:03:19.555 --> 00:03:21.761
No, the cleaning is the bigger issue.

56
00:03:22.448 --> 00:03:23.714
That is the real point:

57
00:03:23.842 --> 00:03:28.776
people correct a wrong paraphrase far more readily than they tell it all themselves.

58
00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:35.131
Juniors fill silences out of nerves; the important sentence often comes right after.

59
00:03:36.487 --> 00:03:40.678
Resistance is usually quiet: short answers, it's in the report.

60
00:03:41.505 --> 00:03:43.885
Name it lightly: what happened last time?

61
00:03:44.899 --> 00:03:49.218
Often a previous consultant left a slide deck, and nothing changed.

62
00:03:49.992 --> 00:03:51.002
Let them tell it.

63
00:03:51.786 --> 00:03:54.561
A notebook feels less like an interrogation.

64
00:03:55.602 --> 00:04:00.582
Leave with five things: the problem in their words, three names, which data,

65
00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:03.450
what success means to the sponsor, and a date.

66
00:04:04.216 --> 00:04:09.011
Within 24 hours on one page: open questions, not guesses.

67
00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:14.909
The line on Thursday with two shift leads, and the summary that same evening.

68
00:04:15.874 --> 00:04:20.100
Closing question: what would make you say in six months that this was worth it?

69
00:04:21.452 --> 00:04:25.957
In a team meeting the senior work preparer says: we did this three years ago.

70
00:04:26.635 --> 00:04:28.295
We just got more forms.

71
00:04:29.341 --> 00:04:31.245
Under pressure you want to defend.

72
00:04:31.998 --> 00:04:36.294
The rule: reflect or paraphrase first, then ask, then answer.

73
00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:40.713
An attack often hides a worry: workload, blame, control.

74
00:04:41.721 --> 00:04:42.963
The mood is tense.

75
00:04:43.695 --> 00:04:46.458
You reflect: it mostly gave you more work.

76
00:04:47.285 --> 00:04:49.085
Then you wait five seconds.

77
00:04:50.144 --> 00:04:52.222
An open question: what happened then?

78
00:04:52.926 --> 00:04:57.338
You paraphrase: so you weren't asked, and the forms came from head office.

79
00:04:58.357 --> 00:04:59.704
I get why you're sceptical.

80
00:05:00.428 --> 00:05:02.576
Will you look at the check with me on Thursday?

81
00:05:03.881 --> 00:05:06.517
Wait three seconds, five under pressure.

82
00:05:07.253 --> 00:05:12.141
Teachers waited about one second; at three, answers got longer and better.

83
00:05:12.855 --> 00:05:16.733
Calm in silence reads as confidence, which helps the youngest.

84
00:05:17.792 --> 00:05:19.197
Open with: what am I missing?

85
00:05:19.926 --> 00:05:21.110
What would have to be true?

86
00:05:21.862 --> 00:05:26.041
No why when it gets heated, it sounds like blame; say: what made you.

87
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.569
No fake question like don't you think: then you learn nothing.

88
00:05:31.618 --> 00:05:35.263
Your experience is a bridge: one sentence, then back.

89
00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:37.338
Check: what did I learn?

90
00:05:38.071 --> 00:05:38.535
Nothing?

91
00:05:39.328 --> 00:05:40.826
Then you talked too much.
